# FNB health watch — CFO 2026-08-04, switched OFF 2026-08-23, back ON 2026-09-13.
#
#   1. Bank link up<->down: email CFO + Kago + Pako on a state change.
#   2. Stuck EFT batches: email when a batch has sat unconfirmed too long.
#
# WHY IT WAS TURNED OFF, AND WHAT CHANGED. On 2026-08-23 the CFO switched this
# off because both alerts were noise. He was right, and for a reason worth
# keeping: FNB answers `425 Too Early` to a poll it has not processed yet, Omni
# recorded every one of those as a FAILURE, and a healthy batch waiting on the
# bank looked exactly like a broken one. 52,617 "failures" in seven days, every
# single one false.
#
# Two things changed before it came back:
#   * A 425 is now recorded as WAITING, not FAILED (PR #949), so the link
#     verdict is no longer computed from tens of thousands of phantom failures.
#   * It runs ONCE A DAY, not hourly, with a 48-hour stuck threshold instead of
#     two hours. A dry run against production on 13-Sep-2026 showed the old
#     settings would have emailed all 46 open batches — some 500 hours old —
#     every single run. An alert that fires every hour about the same 46 things
#     is not an alert, it is a filter people build in Outlook. Once a day, only
#     for a batch genuinely older than two days, is something a person can act
#     on.
#
# The detail belongs to the daily three-way check (fnb-three-way-check.cron),
# which reports WHICH payments disagree with the bank. This job answers only
# "is the link up, and is anything badly stuck".
#
# Reminder: installing this file does NOT schedule it. infra/install-crons.sh
# must run on the box, and fnb-health-watch must be in its ENABLED list.
30 6 * * * root cd /opt/alpha-finance && /usr/bin/docker compose --env-file /etc/alpha-finance/.env exec -T backend python manage.py fnb_health_watch --stuck-hours 48 >> /var/log/fnb-health-watch.log 2>&1
